const P = '/misc/campuses/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Brick, Stone and Concrete — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Thirty university campuses walked through between 2021 and 2026, grouped by region with a map for each, from Harvard and Brown's colonial brick through Collegiate Gothic and Mission-style arcades to Geisel Library's concrete, with USC as home.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2021–2026</p>
  <h1>Brick, Stone and Concrete</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Thirty university campuses we have walked through, region by region.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>30 campuses · 13 states, D.C. and 2 other countries · December 2021 – August 2026</strong><br />
    4 Southern California · 4 Northern California · 3 Pacific Northwest and Vancouver · 3 Texas, Arizona and Las Vegas ·
    5 New England · 4 New York to Washington · 5 Pittsburgh to Chicago · 2 Australia<br />
    Oldest: Harvard, founded 1636 · newest: Cornell Tech, opened 2017 · tallest building: Pitt's Cathedral of
    Learning, 535 feet · most visited: UC San Diego
  </div>

  <p>
    The 2024 road trip post ends with a list of ten campuses that we toured without ever planning to. Seen back to
    back, I wrote there, they make a little history of American campus architecture. Once I went looking, the list
    turned out to be much longer than ten. There were campuses on holidays, campuses on the way to somewhere else,
    campuses on work trips, and campuses we went to for a commencement or an event and stayed to look around. A PhD student
    apparently can't pass a university without walking in.
  </p>
  <p>
    This post collects them by region, each with a map: Southern California first, then up the West Coast to
    Vancouver, across the Southwest to Texas, over to New England, down the East Coast to Washington, and back
    west through Pittsburgh and Ohio to Chicago. Australia comes at the end, and home comes last. Within a region
    they are in the order we visited. Sorted this way, the styles mix: colonial brick, Collegiate Gothic, arcades
    and red tile, Beaux-Arts and concrete often stand a few miles apart. Most of these visits already appear in a
    trip post, and each entry links to it. A few,
    like Caltech, Pepperdine and Arizona State, appear here for the first time. The first few,
    in 2021 and the spring of 2022, were mine (one of them with a friend); from then on, "we" means the two of us, and the one campus my wife
    first saw on her own is marked as such.
  </p>

  <p class="toc"><strong>By region:</strong> <a href="#socal">Southern California</a> · <a href="#norcal">Northern California</a> ·
    <a href="#northwest">Pacific Northwest and Vancouver</a> · <a href="#southwest">Texas, Arizona and Las Vegas</a> ·
    <a href="#new-england">New England</a> · <a href="#new-york-dc">New York, Philadelphia and Washington</a> ·
    <a href="#midwest">Pittsburgh, Ohio and Chicago</a> · <a href="#australia">Australia</a> · <a href="#home">Home</a></p>

  <h2 id="socal">Southern California</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Closest to home. In the West the colleges were younger, and they borrowed from a different past. Instead of Oxford they
    looked to the missions and the Mediterranean: thick walls, round arches, deep shade and red tile roofs.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-socal.webp'} alt="Map of Southern California from Malibu to San Diego marking 4 campuses with photo pins: 1 Caltech, 2 UC San Diego, 3 Pepperdine, 4 UC Irvine." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in Southern California: 1 Caltech, 2 UC San Diego, 3 Pepperdine, 4 UC Irvine.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3 id="caltech">1. Caltech · December 2021</h3>
  <p>
    The earliest visit in this post. Caltech began in 1891 as Throop University, a small school in Pasadena, and
    took its present name in 1920. Bertram Goodhue planned the campus in a Mediterranean style of pale walls,
    arcades and tile roofs. On the morning of December 22nd, 2021, I walked past the glass Chen Neuroscience
    Research Building and on to the
    <strong>Beckman Institute</strong>, where a long reflecting pool leads up to a row of ivy-covered arches under a
    carved stone shell.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caltech.webp'} alt="A long rectangular reflecting pool leading to a pale Mediterranean-style building with ivy-covered arches and a carved shell ornament, between pine trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Beckman Institute and its pool, December 22, 2021, 11:08 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. UC San Diego · February 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The most famous building in this post may be <strong>Geisel Library</strong> at UC San Diego. It was designed by
    William Pereira and opened in 1970: a stack of glass floors on huge concrete legs, like a hand holding books up to
    the sky. It was renamed in 1995 for Theodor Geisel, better known as Dr. Seuss, and his wife Audrey. We walked the
    campus on the afternoon of February 26th, 2023, under big white clouds. A few minutes away, on the roof of the
    stepped concrete engineering building, a little blue house hangs off the edge seven stories up. It is
    <em>Fallen Star</em> by Do Ho Suh, installed in 2012, and it is tilted just enough to make you dizzy.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ucsd-geisel.webp'} alt="Geisel Library, a brutalist building of stacked glass floors on angled concrete columns, against a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Geisel Library, February 26, 2023, 3:47 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ucsd-fallen-star.webp'} alt="A stepped concrete building with a small blue house perched at an angle on the edge of its roof." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Fallen Star</em>, on the roof, 3:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    UC San Diego is also the one campus here that keeps coming back: there were two more visits, in 2024 and 2025.
    Geisel Library even turns up as a LEGO model at Legoland, in <a href="/misc/san-diego">San Diego</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="pepperdine">3. Pepperdine · February 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Pepperdine was founded in 1937 in South Los Angeles and moved in 1972 to a hillside above the Pacific in Malibu,
    which may be the most unfair campus site in the country. The buildings are cream-colored with red tile roofs, and
    almost every one of them looks at the ocean. On the afternoon of February 10th, 2024, from the top of the hill,
    the red roofs and playing fields ran down toward the coast, and the sea went on to a line of hills on the
    horizon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pepperdine.webp'} alt="View from a hillside over red-roofed campus buildings and green playing fields down to a wide blue ocean, with distant hills on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Down the hill to the Pacific, February 10, 2024, 1:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>4. UC Irvine · June 2025</h3>
  <p>
    UC Irvine I know mainly as the place we went for a family member's commencement at the Bren Events Center in June 2025.
    UC Irvine opened in 1965, and its campus was planned by William Pereira, the architect of Geisel Library, as a
    ring of buildings around a central park. That is one architect behind two very different campuses in this post,
    both of them in Southern California.
  </p>

  <h2 id="norcal">Northern California</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Around 1900, American universities started to plan whole campuses at once, in the Beaux-Arts manner: axes,
    vistas, monumental buildings in white stone, and a tower at the end of the view. Berkeley is one; Stanford's
    arcades are the other western model; Santa Cruz is concrete in the redwoods.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-norcal.webp'} alt="Map of the San Francisco Bay Area and Santa Cruz marking 4 campuses with photo pins: 1 UC Berkeley, 2 Stanford, 3 San José State, 4 UC Santa Cruz." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in Northern California: 1 UC Berkeley, 2 Stanford, 3 San José State, 4 UC Santa Cruz.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. UC Berkeley · May 2022</h3>
  <p>
    Berkeley was founded in 1868, and John Galen Howard, its supervising architect from 1901, gave it its white granite
    Beaux-Arts core. At its center is <strong>Sather Tower</strong>, the Campanile, 307 feet tall and finished in
    1914, loosely modeled on the bell tower in St Mark's Square in Venice. Beside it is Doe Library (1911). I walked the
    campus on the morning of May 18th, 2022, from the stadium past the Campanile to Sather Gate and Sproul Plaza, where
    the Free Speech Movement started in 1964. More in <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">Road Trips, 2022</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'berkeley-campanile.webp'} alt="Sather Tower, a tall white granite bell tower, rising above trees against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sather Tower, May 18, 2022.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Stanford · 2022 and 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Stanford opened in 1891. Frederick Law Olmsted did the site plan, and the Boston firm of Shepley, Rutan and
    Coolidge designed the <strong>Main Quad</strong>, mixing Romanesque arches with Spanish mission red tile. The
    result is the campus look that most of the West later copied: long arcades of rough buff sandstone, deep shade
    under the arches and dazzling sun on the courtyard. I spent a full day there on May 21st, 2022, on a road trip
    with a friend, and walked from the Quad to Hoover Tower (1941) and the Engineering Quad. The
    trip is in <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">Road Trips, 2022</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-arcade.webp'} alt="A long sandstone arcade of Romanesque arches in the Main Quad, with sunlight on the floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arcades of the Main Quad, May 2022.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    I came back with my wife on the Saturday after Thanksgiving 2023, and we walked from the Main Quad to Memorial
    Church, whose façade is a gold mosaic, and on to Hoover Tower. That weekend is in
    <a href="/misc/bay-area#nov-2023">The Bay Area, Again and Again</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanford-memorial-church.webp'} alt="The façade of Stanford Memorial Church: a large mosaic of figures in the gable, a stained-glass window, and a band of gold mosaic with angels over three carved arches, under a deep-blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Memorial Church, November 25, 2023, 2:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. San José State · May 2026</h3>
  <p>
    San José State traces its founding to 1857, which makes it the oldest public university in California, and its
    campus sits in the middle of downtown San Jose. I walked over on the evening of May 23rd, 2026, during my summer
    in the Bay Area, and came to the pair of gateway towers at Fourth and San Carlos Streets, where a banner across
    the gate read "Congratulations Graduates!" A few minutes later, at seven, the low sun lit the Event Center
    between two palm trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sjsu-gateway.webp'} alt="Two square stone gateway towers with pyramid roofs at a campus entrance, a blue banner reading Congratulations Graduates strung between them, palm trees and a glass tower behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The gateway at Fourth and San Carlos, May 23, 2026, 6:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sjsu-event-center.webp'} alt="A tan arena building with a diamond motif on its facade, two tall palm trees in front, in low evening sun under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Event Center, 7:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>4. UC Santa Cruz · August 2026</h3>
  <p>
    UC Santa Cruz opened in 1965, on the hill above the town, and it was built into the redwood forest
    rather than in place of it: colleges, labs and libraries stand among the trees, many of them in plain concrete.
    My wife was there first, on her own with Wangcai, on the afternoon of August 11th, 2026. Her photos show
    concrete buildings with deep-set windows under redwoods taller than they are. Two weeks later, on the last day
    of <a href="/misc/tahoe-2026">the Tahoe trip</a>, we drove through the campus together, down through the dry
    grassland with Monterey Bay below, and a plush banana slug, the university's mascot, rode home on the dashboard.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ucsc-redwoods.webp'} alt="A pale concrete building with deep-set windows behind tall redwoods and a young tree, with a concrete picnic table in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Concrete among the redwoods, August 11, 2026, 3:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="northwest">The Pacific Northwest and Vancouver</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Seattle, Vancouver and Eugene.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-northwest.webp'} alt="Map of Oregon, Washington and southern British Columbia marking 3 campuses with photo pins: 1 University of Washington, 2 UBC, 3 University of Oregon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in the Pacific Northwest and Vancouver: 1 University of Washington, 2 UBC, 3 University of Oregon.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. University of Washington · June 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The University of Washington was founded in 1861 and moved to its present site in 1895. In 1909 the campus hosted
    the Alaska-Yukon-Pacific Exposition, a world's fair, and the Olmsted Brothers' plan for the fair still shapes the
    middle of the campus. We came through at four o'clock on June 17th, 2023, on a flat gray afternoon, and walked
    along shaded lawns under huge old trees, then out to University Way, "the Ave", with its queues at the drink
    counters. On the way we passed Suzzallo Library, with its row of tall Gothic windows over the arched doors.
    The rest of that trip is in <a href="/misc/pnw-2023#june">Seattle, the Olympics and Portland</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uw-suzzallo.webp'} alt="The Collegiate Gothic façade of Suzzallo Library at the University of Washington: tall traceried windows above arched doorways and wide steps, under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Suzzallo Library, June 17, 2023, 3:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. UBC · September 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The University of British Columbia was founded in 1908 and moved to Point Grey, at the western tip of Vancouver,
    in 1925. It is a big, green, spread-out campus with the sea on three sides. We stopped on our first afternoon in
    Vancouver, September 29th, 2023. In the sun at 3:08 pm, the fountain in Martha Piper Plaza, named for a former
    president, was throwing up its jets above the university's name carved around the basin, and the first trees
    were turning yellow. Later, at 3:45, we passed a carved pole by the path, topped by an eagle with spread wings.
    The rest of that trip is in <a href="/misc/vancouver-2023">Fall Colors from Vancouver to Whistler</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ubc.webp'} alt="A round fountain with many jets in a paved plaza, with THE UNIVERSITY OF BRITISH COLUMBIA carved around the basin, trees and glass buildings behind, in bright sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Martha Piper Plaza, September 29, 2023, 3:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'ubc-pole.webp'} alt="A tall carved and painted wooden pole topped by an eagle with spread wings, against a clear blue sky above trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A carved pole by the path, 3:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. University of Oregon · May 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The University of Oregon opened in Eugene in 1876. We passed through around lunchtime on May 12th, 2024, the
    day after a night drive north to chase an aurora that never came. Our first stop on campus was the student
    recreation center, where a cutout of the Oregon Duck, the university's mascot, stood guard in the lobby in green
    and yellow. Ten minutes later we were outside <strong>Hayward Field</strong>, the university's track stadium,
    with a translucent curved roof over the stands and a slim tower beside it wrapped
    in images of Oregon athletes.
    The rest of the day is in <a href="/misc/springfield-2024">Springfield, Oregon</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oregon-duck.webp'} alt="The bright lobby of a recreation center with a climbing wall in the background and a cutout of the Oregon Duck mascot in green and yellow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Duck at the Rec, May 12, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'oregon-hayward-tower.webp'} alt="A tall, slim silver tower printed with images of athletes and the word OREGON, beside a translucent curved stadium roof, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hayward Field's tower, 12:58 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="southwest">Texas, Arizona and Las Vegas</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">A Beaux-Arts tower in Austin, Victorian brick in Tempe and new architecture in Las Vegas.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-southwest.webp'} alt="Map of the Southwest from Las Vegas to Austin marking 3 campuses with photo pins: 1 UT Austin, 2 Arizona State, 3 UNLV." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in Texas, Arizona and Las Vegas: 1 UT Austin, 2 Arizona State, 3 UNLV.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. UT Austin · July 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Austin's version of the Beaux-Arts tower at the end of the axis is the University of Texas's <strong>Main Building</strong>,
    307 feet tall, the same height as Berkeley's, finished in 1937 to a design by Paul Cret. We came at it the classic
    way, from the south, at about half past four on July 1st, 2023. A wide boulevard runs straight toward it and ends at
    the Littlefield Fountain (1933), with bronze sea horses charging out of a pool the color of a postcard. The trip is
    in <a href="/misc/texas-2023">Texas, 2023</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-tower-boulevard.webp'} alt="The University of Texas tower at the end of a wide boulevard, with the Littlefield Fountain and live oaks in front, under a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The approach from the south, July 1, 2023, 4:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ut-tower-fountain.webp'} alt="The University of Texas tower rising straight up behind the bronze sea horses of the Littlefield Fountain, with live oaks and white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tower over the fountain, 4:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Arizona State · March 2025</h3>
  <p>
    Arizona State started in 1885 as the Territorial Normal School, a teachers' college in the desert town of Tempe.
    Its oldest surviving building is <strong>Old Main</strong>, finished in 1898: three stories of red brick with
    steep gables and a deep porch, now standing in the middle of one of the largest universities in the country. We
    came by at a quarter past five on March 21st, 2025, when the low sun turned the brick almost orange against a
    hard blue sky. The rest of that trip is in <a href="/misc/phoenix-2025">An Inverted Pyramid, a Waymo and a City of Vaults</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'asu-old-main.webp'} alt="Old Main, a red-brick building with steep gables and a porch, behind a long ARIZONA STATE UNIVERSITY sign and palm trees, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Old Main (1898), March 21, 2025, 5:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. UNLV · August 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The University of Nevada, Las Vegas began in 1957 as a branch of the state university.
    We walked across the campus with Wangcai at dusk on August 31st, 2025. The building to see is <strong>Hospitality Hall</strong>, home of the William F. Harrah
    College of Hospitality: pale stacked boxes and
    dark glass cantilevered over the entrance, with a curtain of rippled metal hanging in front of it. Elsewhere on
    campus the university's initials stand in giant red letters, and Wangcai sat in front of them. More of that
    weekend is in <a href="/misc/las-vegas">Las Vegas</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'unlv-hospitality-hall.webp'} alt="Hospitality Hall at UNLV at dusk: stacked, cantilevered pale and dark boxes over a glass ground floor, with a rippled metal screen at the entrance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hospitality Hall, August 31, 2025, 7:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'unlv-letters.webp'} alt="Wangcai on a pink leash sitting in front of giant red letters spelling UNLV, with trees and a lawn behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai at the UNLV letters, 7:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="new-england">New England</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">The oldest corner of the list: colonial brick and Gothic stone, and in Boston some of the newest glass. Most of it
    is from two days of the 2024 road trip.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-new-england.webp'} alt="Map of southern New England marking 5 campuses with photo pins: 1 Yale, 2 Brown, 3 Harvard, 4 MIT, 5 Boston University." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in New England: 1 Yale, 2 Brown, 3 Harvard, 4 MIT, 5 Boston University.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Yale · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Yale is where the Gothic look of the American campus reached its peak. Much of the central campus was built in
    the 1920s and 30s by one architect, James Gamble Rogers, who went to great lengths to make new buildings look
    centuries old. We walked through at midday on August 2nd, 2024, past Sterling Memorial Library, which is
    essentially a cathedral dedicated to books, and the brick tower of the Humanities Quadrangle above its stone
    archway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'yale-hq.webp'} alt="Yale's Humanities Quadrangle, a Gothic stone building with a tall brick tower above an arched entrance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tower of the Humanities Quadrangle, August 2, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Brown · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Brown was founded in 1764, the seventh college in the colonies, and it still looks the part. We climbed College
    Hill late on the afternoon of August 2nd, 2024, came in through Faunce Arch onto the Main Green, and found the
    whole campus in red brick and white trim, like a New England town common. <strong>University Hall</strong>,
    built in 1770, is the oldest building on campus. During the Revolutionary War it was used as a barracks and
    hospital for American and French troops. Round the corner on Waterman Street, the bronze Brown Bear stands on his
    hind legs. The full day is in <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">the 2024 road trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brown-university-hall.webp'} alt="University Hall, a four-story red-brick Georgian building with a cupola, across Brown's Main Green." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>University Hall (1770), August 2, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. Harvard · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The next morning we were in Cambridge. Harvard is the oldest of them all: it was founded in 1636, and its
    Yard is still lined with the plain brick halls of the colonial college. We walked around it on August 3rd and
    had a gelato. I don't have a single photo of the Yard to show for it. It is the one campus in this post that
    left only a parking charge and a dessert behind, and I have included it anyway.
  </p>

  <h3>4. MIT · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    MIT moved across the Charles to Cambridge in 1916, into a single connected complex of white limestone buildings
    under a great dome, planned as one in the Beaux-Arts manner. We only drove through, on August 3rd, 2024, past the
    labs and startups of Kendall Square, with the Boston skyline lined up across the river. It was not a proper visit.
    Six months later, on a freezing January night, we walked out onto the Harvard Bridge, which runs from Back Bay
    to MIT, and that is a story for <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">another post</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Boston University · January 2025</h3>
  <p>
    Boston University is strung out for more than a mile along Commonwealth Avenue, with the Green Line trolleys running
    down the middle of the road. We drove through at eleven in the morning on January 25th, 2025, past the new
    <strong>Center for Computing &amp; Data Sciences</strong>, opened in 2022, a stack of glass boxes set
    slightly off-center like a game of Jenga. On Bay State Road it rises right behind a Victorian stone mansion,
    the two of them more than a century apart. From <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">the January 2025 trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'bu-data-sciences.webp'} alt="BU's stacked glass tower rising behind an ornate Victorian stone mansion with bay windows, above a snowy bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Old Boston and new BU, January 25, 2025.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="new-york-dc">New York, Philadelphia and Washington</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Three campuses from New Year's 2023 on the East Coast, and Syracuse from the 2024 road trip.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-new-york-dc.webp'} alt="Map of New York, Pennsylvania and the Washington area marking 4 campuses with photo pins: 1 Cornell Tech, 2 Penn, 3 Georgetown, 4 Syracuse." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in New York, Philadelphia and Washington: 1 Cornell Tech, 2 Penn, 3 Georgetown, 4 Syracuse.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Cornell Tech · January 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The newest campus in the post. Cornell Tech is a graduate school on Roosevelt Island in the East River, built on
    the site of an old hospital and opened in 2017. We walked south from the tram station on the evening of New Year's
    Day, 2023, and found the angular glass Tata Innovation Center lit from inside on an empty plaza, with Midtown
    across the water. From <a href="/misc/newyear-2023">New Year's on the East Coast</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cornell-tech.webp'} alt="The angular glass Tata Innovation Center on the Cornell Tech campus lit from inside at night, an empty plaza in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cornell Tech after hours, January 1, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Penn · January 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The University of Pennsylvania goes back to Benjamin Franklin's academy of the 1740s. Its spine is
    <strong>Locust Walk</strong>, the brick path through the middle of campus. We walked it on the evening of January 2nd, 2023, in the rain, during the winter break, when the campus was almost
    empty. Strings of lights hung between the trees, and halfway along stood <em>Covenant</em>, a big red steel
    sculpture of tilted cylinders by Alexander Liberman, from 1975. More in
    <a href="/misc/newyear-2023">New Year's on the East Coast</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'penn-covenant.webp'} alt="A large red steel sculpture of tilted cylinders straddling a wet brick path on the University of Pennsylvania campus at night, lampposts glowing in the mist." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Covenant</em> on Locust Walk, January 2, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. Georgetown · January 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Two days later, on the last morning of the same trip, we walked onto the Georgetown campus. Georgetown was founded
    in 1789, the same year as the Constitution took effect, and it is the oldest Catholic university in the United
    States. The campus sits on a hill above the Potomac. At 8:49 on a gray January morning, the view from the
    top went out over brick roofs and bare trees to the river and the glass towers of Rosslyn on the Virginia side.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'georgetown-view.webp'} alt="A gray morning view from a rooftop over Georgetown brick roofs and bare trees to the Potomac River and the towers of Rosslyn beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Across the Potomac to Rosslyn, January 4, 2023, 8:49 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>4. Syracuse · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Syracuse University was founded in 1870 and sits on a hill above the city. Its first building, the
    <strong>Hall of Languages</strong> (1873), is a pale stone Second Empire building with mansard roofs and towers,
    the kind of thing Victorians built when they wanted a college to look serious. We crossed the campus at golden
    hour on August 4th, 2024, with sprinklers ticking on the lawns and the place nearly empty. Hendricks Chapel, domed
    and red-brick, was catching the last of the sun. From <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">the 2024 road trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'syracuse-hall-of-languages.webp'} alt="Syracuse University's Hall of Languages, a pale stone Second Empire building with towers, in evening sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hall of Languages (1873), August 4, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="midwest">Pittsburgh, Ohio and Chicago</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Four days of the 2024 road trip, from a Gothic skyscraper to a Gehry and back to Gothic again.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-midwest.webp'} alt="Map of the Great Lakes region from Pittsburgh to Chicago marking 5 campuses with photo pins: 1 Pitt, 2 Carnegie Mellon, 3 Ohio State, 4 Case Western Reserve, 5 UChicago." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in Pittsburgh, Ohio and Chicago: 1 Pitt, 2 Carnegie Mellon, 3 Ohio State, 4 Case Western Reserve, 5 UChicago.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Pitt · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Pittsburgh took Yale's Gothic idea and stood it on end. The <strong>Cathedral of Learning</strong> at the University
    of Pittsburgh, designed by Charles Klauder and dedicated in 1937, is forty-two stories and 535 feet of limestone, the
    tallest educational building in the Western Hemisphere. Its Nationality Rooms are classrooms, each designed in the
    style of a different country by the city's immigrant communities. We stood under it in the early afternoon of
    August 6th, 2024, with fast clouds going over.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pitt-cathedral.webp'} alt="The Cathedral of Learning, a very tall limestone Gothic tower, against a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Cathedral of Learning, August 6, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Carnegie Mellon · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    A few blocks from the Cathedral of Learning is Carnegie Mellon, founded by Andrew Carnegie in 1900 as the Carnegie
    Technical Schools. On August 6th, 2024, its glassy <strong>Cohon University Center</strong> was quiet in the August
    heat. In one afternoon in Pittsburgh you can walk from 1930s Gothic to 1990s glass in ten minutes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'cmu.webp'} alt="The modern glass entrance of the Cohon University Center at Carnegie Mellon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Cohon University Center, August 6, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. Ohio State · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Ohio State was founded in 1870 as a land-grant college and is now one of the largest universities in the country.
    That same evening, August 6th, we walked up Neil Avenue onto the campus as the lights came on: lit windows, empty
    paths, and a building whose red glass front and skybridge glowed like a lantern.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'osu-night.webp'} alt="A modern building with a glowing red glass facade and skybridge on the Ohio State campus at nightfall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ohio State at nightfall, August 6, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>4. Case Western Reserve · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The next morning, August 7th, we walked around Case Western Reserve in Cleveland and found Frank Gehry's
    <strong>Peter B. Lewis Building</strong>, the business school, which opened in 2002. Its brick walls bulge and
    lean, and great sheets of stainless steel pour off the roof as if the building were melting from the top down. It
    is the strangest building on any campus here, and the closest thing to a sculpture.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cwru-gehry.webp'} alt="Close-up of Frank Gehry's Peter B. Lewis Building: curving brick walls under a swooping steel overhang." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gehry's Peter B. Lewis Building, August 7, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>5. The University of Chicago · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The University of Chicago was founded in 1890 with John D. Rockefeller's money, and it was built from the start
    as a Gothic campus of gray limestone quadrangles, a piece of Oxford set down on the South Side. We got to Hyde Park
    just after eight in the evening on August 9th, 2024, in time to see a Gothic chapel on the campus lit up gold by
    floodlights in the trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uchicago-chapel.webp'} alt="A Gothic stone chapel at the University of Chicago, lit gold by floodlights at dusk, framed by a tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A chapel in Hyde Park at dusk, August 9, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="australia">Australia</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Two campuses on two evenings, during SIGGRAPH Asia.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-australia.webp'} alt="Map of southeastern Australia marking 2 campuses with photo pins: 1 University of Sydney, 2 ANU." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Campuses in Australia: 1 University of Sydney, 2 ANU.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1–2. The University of Sydney and ANU · December 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Two campuses in Australia, during SIGGRAPH Asia. On the evening of December 14th, 2023, we walked a couple of
    kilometers south to the University of Sydney, which was founded in 1850 and is the oldest university in the country. I
    have no photo of the campus itself, but my wife does. At 7:19 pm the sandstone Quadrangle was gold in the low sun.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'usyd-quadrangle.webp'} alt="The sandstone Gothic Revival Quadrangle of the University of Sydney with its pinnacled clock tower, lit by the low sun across a green lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Quadrangle, December 14, 2023, 7:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'usyd-doorway.webp'} alt="A sandstone Gothic façade with tall traceried windows, a statue on the roof and a pointed-arch wooden doorway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A sandstone doorway nearby, 7:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The next evening, in Canberra, we walked across the
    <strong>Australian National University</strong>, founded in 1946 as a research university for the new nation and
    spread out along the lake at the foot of Black Mountain. One new building had a façade of white hexagons, each
    framing a window, like a honeycomb tipped on its side. More in
    <a href="/misc/siggraph-asia-2023">the SIGGRAPH Asia post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'anu-letters.webp'} alt="Giant white letters spelling ANU on a lawn under tall poplars in evening sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The ANU letters, December 15, 2023, 6:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'anu-honeycomb.webp'} alt="A modern building at ANU with a façade of white interlocking hexagonal window frames." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A honeycomb façade at ANU, December 15, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="home">Coda: home</h2>
  <p>
    And then there is the campus we see every week. USC was founded in 1880, when Los Angeles was a town of about
    eleven thousand people, and its core is red brick in a Romanesque style. On September 19th, 2023, the camera came
    out on campus for once, as if it were somewhere new. In the afternoon it was the Henry Salvatori Computer Science
    Center, all concrete and brick. In the evening it was USC Village, which opened in 2017
    and is built of brick in a Gothic style, with pointed arches, a clock and a spire. Seen at dusk with the fountain
    running, it is hard to believe it is the same age as Cornell Tech's glass.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'usc-salvatori.webp'} alt="The white concrete fascia of the Henry Salvatori Computer Science Center with its name in metal letters, under a blue sky with pine trees and a taller building behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Salvatori Computer Science Center, September 19, 2023, 2:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'usc-village.webp'} alt="A large brick building with Gothic arches and a tall pointed spire above a plaza with a fountain and café umbrellas, at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>USC Village at dusk, 6:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That is the history of campus architecture in one walk. A university that is not yet 150 years old built
    a Gothic village after 2010, and it works for the same reason the rest of the list works: when a university
    builds, it builds to look as if it has always been there. Yale reportedly aged its stone with acid, Stanford borrowed the
    missions, and USC borrowed Oxford. Only the concrete ones, Geisel Library and its friends, ever tried to look
    like the future. Half a century on, they are a period style too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writing this, I also noticed how few of these visits were planned. Most of them were in the gaps: an hour before a
    flight, an evening after a conference, a stop on the way to dinner. A campus is one of the few places you can walk
    into almost anywhere in the world, without a ticket, and feel at home for an hour. Maybe that is why we keep
    walking in.
  </p>
